---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Field from '../components/Field.astro';
import Terminal from '../components/Terminal.astro';
import { session } from '../data/dna-session.mjs';
---
<Base title="DNA" description="One program for building, running, governing and recording a Hale application: changes made in a sandbox, proved with the toolchain, reviewed, applied when approved, run, watched, and recorded as commits.">
  <Field />
  <section class="wrap page">
    <div class="page-head">
      <h1>One program for building, running, governing and recording an application.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Code is now written faster than anyone can read it. DNA gives a Hale
        application what a company gives a product: people with final say,
        which DNA calls the Board; a reviewer with limited authority, an
        AI-backed role called the Leader; a delivery pipeline that proposes,
        proves and applies each change; the machines that run it and the
        monitoring that watches it; the models it may call and the budget it
        may spend; and an audit log of every decision. Those are usually four
        systems and a wiki. Here they are one program next to your code,
        with its working state in a database it owns and its history as
        commits in your repository.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What hale dna init writes</h2>
      <p>
        <code>hale dna init</code> generates the organism next to your code and
        leaves the application untouched. What it writes is the organization as
        source: a purpose and a charter, the Board and the Leader and the grant
        between them, the models and the daily budget, the work it accepts and
        the workflows that carry it, and a law the compiler holds against the
        wiring you actually built. It also writes what the organism runs on and
        what watches it. One program carries the whole loop from then on. You
        ask for something in a sentence, from a terminal, another clone,
        GitHub, or the page it serves. The substrate copies the repository,
        lets a model edit under a grant that is read, edit, format and check
        and nothing more, and commits the candidate there. The toolchain then
        checks, verifies and tests it, composes the fleet it would deploy,
        diffs it against the program's structure, rehearses rolling it back,
        and keeps every output under its own hash.
      </p>
      <p>
        One review, three views, pinned to one commit: the source diff, the
        structural diff, the evidence. The Leader decides inside the grant, a
        model reading those views and leaving its reasoning on the record.
        The Board decides outside the grant, or when the law, an effect or
        the fleet's shape moves, and the Board is people. Approval is a
        commit. It is expressed, restarted here or redeployed by every node
        the plan assigns, and watched. An instance that falls over inside the observation window rolls the change back everywhere, and the record names the instance.
      </p>
      <p>
        The working state lives in a database the organism owns, under its own
        roles; the nerves are a stream; the vault holds its secrets. The
        history is <code>refs/dna/journal</code>: one commit per event, in the
        repository the code lives in, so a teammate with a clone has the whole
        story and a pull request can be the review. The organism grows the
        way the code does. A new responsibility, a practice or a wider grant is
        a proposal, a candidate commit, a verdict and a restart, so the record
        is the organization.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose wide" style="margin-top:var(--s8)"><h2 style="margin-top:0">What one organism holds</h2></div>
    <ul class="facts">
      <li><b>The organization</b><p>Purpose and charter. The Board, people with final authority. The Leader, a model-backed position that decides inside a grant the Board wrote. Practices, proposed and settled as Board reviews. All of it is source in <code>dna/org</code>.</p></li>
      <li><b>Delivery</b><p>An ask becomes a task. A sandbox makes the change. The toolchain formats, checks, verifies and tests it, composes the fleet it would deploy, diffs the program's structure and rehearses the rollback. A review settles it, inside or outside the grant, or as a pull request. Approval applies it.</p></li>
      <li><b>Operations</b><p>Expression on your machine or across a fleet, with a plan naming each service's seed and node and <code>hale node</code> running one. Senses and reflexes watching it. Roles per client on every backend, secrets in a vault, deployment profiles. A crash inside the observation window rolls back on every node it reached.</p></li>
      <li><b>Business operations</b><p>Which models each position may call and a budget per day, probed by <code>hale dna models</code>. Spend reserved, settled and compensated under a grant's windows. Receipts filed as customer or confidential and read only as recorded rows. Schedules for recurring work.</p></li>
      <li><b>Working state</b><p>Memory in a database the organism owns, the nerves as a stream, the vault for secrets, brought up together by <code>hale dna dev</code>.</p></li>
      <li><b>History</b><p><code>refs/dna/journal</code>, one commit per event: the ask, the sandbox, every model call with its cost and digests, each check with its receipt, the verdict with a name on it, the apply, the deploy, the outcome. <code>hale dna history m1</code> is one change's whole story, from any clone.</p></li>
    </ul>

    <div class="prose wide" style="margin-top:var(--s12)">
      <h2 style="margin-top:0">One session</h2>
      <p class="muted">The demo application, with the editor on scripted models so the transcript is reproducible. A hosted model gives the same commands and the same record.</p>
    </div>
    <Terminal code={session} kind="hale dna" />
    <div class="prose">
      <p style="margin-top:var(--s6)">
        That is the daily surface: <code>ask</code>, <code>review</code>, <code>status</code>, <code>history</code>, <code>board</code>. A team
        sets <code>git config dna.github</code>, which turns every pending review
        into a pull request and every GitHub review into a verdict. A fleet
        adds a plan that names each service's seed and node; <code>hale node</code> runs a node, and an approval redeploys the instances it touched. The
        rest is how much the Leader may decide and which models it uses: a
        grant and a router in a file you own.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose wide" style="margin-top:var(--s12)">
      <h2 style="margin-top:0">Why, in 2026</h2>
    </div>
    <ul class="facts">
      <li><b>More changes than you can read</b><p>Agents produce pull requests faster than reviewers absorb them, so review degrades to skimming and CI becomes the only real check. DNA does not make reading unnecessary. It makes each change one thing, already verified, and explains it twice: the source diff, and what changed in the program's shape. A rule that flips is one line in that view and a page of archaeology in the source. It also puts a first reader on every change whose reasoning you can audit: the Leader's verdict is a model call with its digests and its cost on the record, inside a grant only the Board can widen.</p></li>
      <li><b>Autonomy as a checkbox</b><p>Today an agent's permissions are a toggle in a tool: edit files, run commands, auto-approve. Here the boundary is a constitution the compiler enforces. The editing position cannot reach git, the network, a deployment or the organization's memory; the Leader decides and never commits; and <code>hale check</code> holds that against the org chart you wrote. Hand the editor a git handle and the build fails, with the path that shows why. Widening the grant is a reviewed commit, like any other change.</p></li>
      <li><b>Agents edit repositories; systems run</b><p>A change is usually judged by tests, in a checkout, with no idea what is running where. Hale programs are modeled by their compiler, so a candidate is measured on what it does to that model and composed as the fleet it would deploy. A change to one service that breaks a claim over all of them is denied before a person sees a line. Every instance reports the model hash it expresses into the record, so <code>hale dna fleet</code> can say which replica runs which version.</p></li>
      <li><b>Provenance is a chat log</b><p>Why a change happened is usually reconstructed from PR descriptions and transcripts. DNA's record has the ask, the sandbox, every model call with its cost and digests (never the prompt), each check with its receipt, the verdict with a name on it, the apply, the deploy and the outcome, as commits next to the code, synced like code. <code>hale dna history m1</code> is one change's whole story, from any clone.</p></li>
      <li><b>Rollback is a hope</b><p>A change that breaks production is reverted by whoever notices. Here a change that crashes one instance inside the observation window is reset to the commit it started from, on every node it reached, mechanically. A change you reject after the fact goes the same way. A retried apply reads its own record and never commits twice.</p></li>
      <li><b>Where it runs</b><p>In the <code>hale</code> binary, on your workstation, with your model key or a local model, with git as the only shared service. No control plane.</p></li>
    </ul>

    <div class="prose wide" style="margin-top:var(--s12)" id="habitat">
      <h2 style="margin-top:0">The habitat <span class="ev">in development</span></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        An organism looks after one application and its record. People
        rarely work inside just one. The same person answers to a team, a
        company and a customer, and firms cooperate on shared work without
        sharing their books. The habitat is the layer for that: the records,
        tools, channels and practices that several organisms and the people
        in them share, and the rules for sharing them. One effort's organism
        can be the habitat of several others, and the relationships between
        them need not form a tree. It never requires incorporation, control
        of an employer's systems, or a new accounting platform.
      </p>
    </div>
    <ul class="facts" style="margin-top:var(--s6)">
      <li><b>A record is the unit of isolation</b><p>A repository and its record, with the backends attached to it: memory, nerves, secrets, senses. A clone is a replica of a record and carries no access of its own. Moving or cloning a record keeps its identity and provenance.</p></li>
      <li><b>Sharing is deliberate publication</b><p>Two firms keep their private records and agree on a third, shared one. They publish selected facts into it, each carrying its origin, lineage and purpose. Syncing a whole record cannot export part of a private history, so a shared record never receives one.</p></li>
      <li><b>Visibility is enforced</b><p>Whoever holds a full replica can read it, and a projection that filters rows cannot hide them. Sensitive material therefore lives outside the replicated record, as protected evidence in the record's own memory, read only through a principal-checked boundary. That covers its derivatives too: summaries, embeddings, prompts, the context a worker is given.</p></li>
      <li><b>Identity is bound at every entry</b><p>The face, the API, the command line, a synced decision, a callback, resumed work. Each checks the actor, the record, the current membership and the policy. A git author, a <code>--as</code> flag or a position in a view is attribution only; proof comes from the check at the entry.</p></li>
      <li><b>Three independent choices</b><p>Participation: one person, a team on one record, or firms cooperating through a shared record. Deployment: your machine, hosted behind a domain, or another admitted placement. Principal source: explicit local trust, or a verified identity provider. A hosted single user with an identity provider is a normal first profile.</p></li>
      <li><b>Authority as part of the graph</b><p>The compiler knows who owns a locus and what it can reach. The design adds on whose authority it runs: <code>runs_under(locus, principal)</code>, closed the way ownership is closed. A vault policy, a database role or a broker account then becomes the rendered reach of one principal, which nobody writes by hand, and a credential is cut for one attachment, short-lived and narrowed, never for the seat.</p></li>
      <li><b>Responsibilities grow; equipment equips them</b><p>A new responsibility is a child the organism proposes from evidence of a need and the authority admits. Standard equipment gives it competence: research and decision briefs, qualification and offers, implementation and release, onboarding and support, receipt capture and reconciliation, publication and experiments, access and documents, deployment and recovery. Installing equipment creates no department, appoints no performer and grants no authority.</p></li>
    </ul>

    <div class="prose wide" style="margin-top:var(--s8)">
      <h2 style="margin-top:0">What exists today</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="tbl-wrap">
    <table class="tbl">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">piece of the design</th><th scope="col">in the tree today</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="wrap">A record per repository, never rewound</td><td class="wrap">Yes. <a href="/docs/dna/memory">Memory and the record</a>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="wrap">Deliberate publication between records</td><td class="wrap">Connections and handoffs of single facts with origin, lineage and purpose: <code>hale dna connect</code>, <code>hale dna handoff</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="wrap">Protected evidence outside the replicated record</td><td class="wrap">Receipts filed as <code>customer</code> or <code>confidential</code> are kept in memory alone, and every read is a row: <code>hale dna receipt</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="wrap">Deployment profiles</td><td class="wrap"><code>hale dna new --profile local | remote-body</code>, <code>hale dna profile</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="wrap">A verified principal source</td><td class="wrap">An OIDC-signed head: <code>git config dna.principal oidc</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="wrap">Roles per client on each backend</td><td class="wrap">Memory's roles, one broker account per role and per attached application, and vault slots.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="wrap">Money reserved against grants</td><td class="wrap">Spend reserved, settled and compensated under a grant's windows.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="wrap"><code>runs_under</code>, policies as projections, per-attachment credentials</td><td class="wrap"><b>Not built.</b> Reserved as a relation for the compiler's registry.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="wrap">Standard equipment</td><td class="wrap"><b>Not built.</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="wrap">Finance capture and books</td><td class="wrap"><b>Not built.</b> No automated payment is part of the first slice.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>
    <div class="prose"><p style="margin-top:var(--s4)" class="muted">Nothing in the design is behaviour to rely on today unless the table says so. <a href="/docs/habitat">The habitat, as designed →</a></p></div>

    <div class="prose wide" style="margin-top:var(--s12)">
      <h2 style="margin-top:0">What it needs, and what it does not claim</h2>
    </div>
    <ul class="facts">
      <li><b>git</b><p>The record lives in the repository, so <code>new</code> and <code>init</code> make one when there is none. Alone on one machine no remote is needed. A team, or nodes, need one.</p></li>
      <li><b>docker compose</b><p><code>hale dna dev</code> brings up the organism's private services with it: Postgres for its working state, which DNA calls memory; NATS for messaging between its parts, the nerves; Prometheus for monitoring, the senses.</p></li>
      <li><b>A model</b><p>A key in the vault, <code>ollama</code> on your PATH, or a coding harness (<code>claude</code>, else <code>codex</code>) on it. The organism still runs without one; the Leader cannot decide anything, and every review waits for you.</p></li>
      <li><b>Every change waits</b><p>Each waits for a verdict: the Leader's, inside a grant a person wrote, or the Board's. A restart is a restart. Live state does not carry across.</p></li>
      <li><b>A blind spot</b><p>The structural view cannot see what a handler computes. A <code>+1</code> becoming <code>+100</code> is invisible to it, which is why the source diff is never optional, for people and for the Leader alike. The design was gated on a test of exactly that: reviewers deciding from the structural diff alone, the source alone, and both. The combined view is what shipped, and the structural view is credited with what it earned. It decides shape and rules, and it says when it cannot decide.</p></li>
      <li><b>The habitat</b><p>Designed and partly built. <a href="#habitat">The table above</a> says which pieces are in the tree; the rest is not behaviour to rely on yet.</p></li>
    </ul>

    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:var(--s12)">
      <h2 style="margin-top:0">Start</h2>
      <div class="install" role="group" aria-label="Start DNA">
        <span class="sigil" aria-hidden="true">$</span>
        <span class="cmd">hale dna init . &amp;&amp; hale dna dev</span>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:var(--s4)">
        The <a href="/docs/dna">organism section of the guide</a> reads the
        organism first: the map of its parts, getting started, one task end to
        end, then one chapter per part, and shaping and governing it. The
        building block underneath, with the record as a git branch, the
        review's pin and the autonomy rules, is <a href="/docs/dna/dna">DNA, the building block</a>. The design it
        implements is <a href="https://github.com/hale-lang/hale/issues/566">Hale DNA, Phase 3</a>,
        on the <a href="https://github.com/hale-lang/hale/issues/521">RFC</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
